{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cm6NZRBQq61Bry1UQYGPer","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL ARR reviewer guidelines scoring rubric 2025 2026\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/cfp.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/cfp.md\"},{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp\"},{\"title\":\"Future Improvements to ARR – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/future\"}]\n\nI found relevant information about the ACL ARR reviewer guidelines and scoring rubric for 2025-2026. Here are the key details:\n\n## Reviewer Scoring System (February 2025 onwards)\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Scoring Requirements\n\nThe soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review. If you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation, and you need to revise it.\n\n## Score Distinction\n\nSometimes you may find the work sound, but not something that is interesting for you personally. Such preferences are more subjective and should be reflected in a separate excitement score, which is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\n## Meta-review Scoring (Area Chairs)\n\nThe ARR metareview score has changed in February 2025 to be more aligned with reviewer scores. Like with reviews, the score should be backed up by the meta-review text. The new options are: 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n\n## Additional Policies\n\nStarting in May 2025, ARR also adopts the new policy on the 'highly irresponsible' (meta-)reviewers. The program chairs and ARR EiCs will consider the egregious cases reported by chairs (on their own judgement or based on author reports), including the cases of extremely terse reviews, unprofessional tone, and LLM-generated reviews.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d7f648b5-736e-406c-b210-cfefb03cc5ed","uuid":"2a47cfe2-644e-43ab-b2d5-71f264d1d91e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T06:53:44.309Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL ARR reviewer guidelines scoring rubric 2025 2026","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01LtUGHWANEZsCpzFP9mw1sM","content":[{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/cfp.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/cfp.md"},{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"CALL FOR PAPERS – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/cfp"},{"title":"Future Improvements to ARR – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/future"}]},"I found relevant information about the ACL ARR reviewer guidelines and scoring rubric for 2025-2026. Here are the key details:\n\n## Reviewer Scoring System (February 2025 onwards)\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Scoring Requirements\n\nThe soundness scores must be justified by the text of the review. If you give a low Soundness score without finding any major faults, this means that your review is not a faithful explanation of your recommendation, and you need to revise it.\n\n## Score Distinction\n\nSometimes you may find the work sound, but not something that is interesting for you personally. Such preferences are more subjective and should be reflected in a separate excitement score, which is orthogonal to the assessment of the soundness of the paper.\n\n## Meta-review Scoring (Area Chairs)\n\nThe ARR metareview score has changed in February 2025 to be more aligned with reviewer scores. Like with reviews, the score should be backed up by the meta-review text. The new options are: 5 = Consider for Award: I think this paper could be considered for an outstanding paper award at an *ACL conference (up to top 2.5% papers).\n\n## Additional Policies\n\nStarting in May 2025, ARR also adopts the new policy on the 'highly irresponsible' (meta-)reviewers. The program chairs and ARR EiCs will consider the egregious cases reported by chairs (on their own judgement or based on author reports), including the cases of extremely terse reviews, unprofessional tone, and LLM-generated reviews."],"durationSeconds":7.8982691829999965}}
